SECTION 1 — MISSION, PRODUCT IDENTITY, AND ANTI-SCOPE-COLLAPSE LAW
===============================================================================

1.1 THE MISSION

Construct the finished Empirium Studio AI Workforce.

The desired product is a persistent, visible, operational AI organization built
on and extending Hermes/Hermes Studio capabilities.

This is intended to become a working personal AI company environment in which
the user can:

- see an organization;
- create and inspect departments;
- maintain persistent named AI employees;
- submit durable projects;
- observe tasks and runs;
- inspect which model performed what work;
- observe QA and review;
- inspect knowledge and learning;
- understand errors and blockers;
- manage approvals;
- see truthful model/cost information where available;
- interact with a high-quality living 2D office;
- close the browser;
- return later;
- find that work continued;
- see exactly what happened while away.

This is not merely an attractive dashboard.

This is not merely Hermes Studio reskinned.

This is not merely a Kanban board with robot graphics.

This is not merely Conductor with persistent labels.

This is not merely a collection of chats.

This is not merely a multi-agent demo.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1.2 PRODUCT EXPERIENCE
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The user should eventually be able to experience the system approximately as
follows:

    User opens Empirium Studio.

    User sees the organization at a glance.

    User sees departments represented as operational organizational units.

    User opens Personal Software.

    User sees permanent employees such as:

        Director
        Research Architect
        Developer / Implementation Engineer
        QA Engineer
        Learning Analyst

    These employees are persistent identities.

    They have inspectable configuration and history.

    Their visible office characters correspond to their real operational
    states.

    User assigns work.

    Director interprets the request.

    A durable Project is created.

    Project becomes Tasks.

    Tasks are assigned.

    Execution Runs occur.

    Free implementation intelligence performs coding where appropriate.

    QA occurs independently.

    Rejected work returns for repair.

    Work integrates only after review.

    Knowledge and learning are retained.

    User can close the browser.

    Work continues.

    User returns.

    Project state, task history, runs, review, evidence, model routing and
    learning remain visible.

That is the type of product being built.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1.3 ABSOLUTE ANTI-SCOPE-COLLAPSE RULE
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Previous builds failed because a broad specification was transformed into a
smaller, easier project.

That must be structurally prevented.

No orchestrator, reviewer, architect or implementer may reduce a mandatory
requirement because it appears:

- technically difficult;
- visually difficult;
- expensive in engineering time;
- poorly supported by current Hermes architecture;
- inconvenient;
- uncertain;
- large;
- advanced;
- unfamiliar;
- likely to take days;
- difficult for a free model.

Difficulty affects:

    DECOMPOSITION

not:

    REQUIREMENT STATUS.

If requirement X is too large for one worker:

    split X into X.1, X.2, X.3

Do not silently replace X with a weaker Y.

