8.7 LONG-CONTEXT FREE MODEL BRIEF

For Inkling/Nemotron-class long-context tasks:

provide:

- an index first;
- explicit questions;
- requested evidence format;
- precise source boundaries.

Do not merely upload an enormous repository and say:

    "figure this out."

Ask targeted retrieval questions.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
8.8 MODEL WITHOUT ENFORCED STRUCTURED OUTPUT

If a route does not enforce response schemas:

never directly deserialize arbitrary returned text into privileged actions.

Use:

    text response
       -> extraction
       -> JSON parser
       -> schema validator
       -> deterministic validation
       -> action

If malformed:

request repair or use a local normalization step.

Do not execute shell commands extracted from unvalidated arbitrary output.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
8.9 VISUAL MODEL BRIEF

For screenshot analysis:

include:

- target viewport;
- reference image;
- actual screenshot;
- component name;
- expected interaction;
- requirement IDs;
- what to inspect.

Ask for defect records:

    defect_id
    location
    severity
    observation
    expected
    evidence
    repair criterion

Avoid:

    "Does this look good?"

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
8.10 SONNET REVIEW BRIEF

Use Sonnet when review volume is higher and risk is moderate.

Provide:

- concise requirement;
- diff;
- tests;
- exact behavior;
- suspicious areas.

Ask Sonnet to be adversarial.

Do not let it rewrite code.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
8.11 OPUS REVIEW BRIEF

For Opus, provide primary evidence and ask deeper questions.

Examples:

    "Find architectural violations not covered by tests."

    "Identify any state path where UI can become more authoritative than
    persisted truth."

    "Identify any requirement that this implementation only approximates."

    "Look for restart/retry edge cases."

Opus should spend intelligence on judgment, not rediscovering basic context that
Luna could prepare.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
8.12 SOL DIAGNOSTIC BRIEF

When using Sol as second opinion:

present disagreement explicitly.

Example:

    ARCHITECTURE A
    ARCHITECTURE B
    CONSTRAINTS
    TEST EVIDENCE
    FAILURE
    OPUS POSITION

Ask:

    locate underlying invariant
    identify hidden assumption
    propose bounded resolution

Do not bias it by saying which answer Luna prefers.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
8.13 ASTRA CHALLENGE BRIEF

Astra should usually receive a broad but organized challenge packet.

Use for:

- source specification;
- architecture;
- current implementation summary;
- unresolved issues;
- release evidence.

Ask it to find omissions.

Do not ask Astra to spend time implementing small fixes.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
8.14 CONTEXT COMPACTION

Context compaction may be used.

Never compact away exact:

- requirement language;
- monetary limits;
- security constraints;
- test failures;
- exception messages;
- file paths;
- command output needed for diagnosis;
- model identity;
- evidence refs;
- approval decisions.

Large raw records remain retrievable.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
8.15 HANDOFFS INSTEAD OF TRANSCRIPT FORWARDING

Do not feed each worker full predecessor chats.

Use structured handoffs.

Preserve raw logs separately.

This reduces:

- token waste;
- irrelevant assumptions;
- accidental prompt injection from old model prose;
- context drift.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
8.16 TASK DECOMPOSITION BY MODEL CAPABILITY

Task sizing should reflect worker capability.

Example:

Bad:

    "Build the entire department cockpit."

Better:

    architecture contract frozen

    Free worker A:
        department route + shell

    Free worker B:
        header/status binding

    Free worker C:
        activity rail

    Free worker D:
        project panels

    animation worker:
        office canvas integration

Each independently reviewable.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
8.17 BRIEF QUALITY CHECK

Before dispatch, Luna manager checks:

    Can this worker identify exactly what success means?

    Does it know what not to change?

    Does it know the source contract?

    Does it know how to prove the work?

    Is this task small enough for the selected model?

If not:

rewrite the brief before consuming quota.


===============================================================================
SECTION 9 — DETERMINISTIC SUPERVISOR INSTALLATION AND BOOTSTRAP GATE
===============================================================================

9.1 SUPERVISOR FIRST

Before large product implementation:

install and prove the deterministic supervisor.

The user has already copied the supplied supervisor package onto the VPS.

Search:

    "/home/ash/Desktop/AI Taskforce"

recursively.

Likely identifying filenames include:

    supervisor.mjs
    supervisor.config.example.json
    supervisor.test.mjs
    director-supervisor-contract.md
    empirium-luna-director.example.sh
    *.service

Do not assume exact parent directory.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
9.2 INSPECT BEFORE INSTALL

Review source for:

- network calls;
- shell construction;
- process launching;
- file writes;
- credentials;
- destructive operations;
- expected config;
- tests;
- service assumptions.

Record:

    SUPERVISOR_SOURCE_AUDIT.md

Do not rewrite it from scratch unless audit proves a material defect.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
9.3 COPY, DO NOT MUTATE ORIGINAL

Place active controller copy under TARGET:

    TARGET/AI_WORKFORCE_BUILD/controller/

Keep source package unchanged.

If controller files already exist:

diff them.

Do not blindly overwrite more recent work.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
9.4 SUPERVISOR CONFIGURATION MUST BE DATA-DRIVEN

Configuration should include concepts such as:

    supervisor_version

    polling_interval

    project list

    project_id

    project_root

    director launch command

    heartbeat path

    heartbeat_timeout

    relaunch cooldown

    maximum restart burst

    manager lease timeout

    retry policies

    provider circuit-breaker policies

    pause flag

    completion gates

    log path

    directive queue

    zero-paid policy

Avoid hardcoding project-specific paths inside core supervisor source when they
belong in configuration.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
9.5 SUPERVISOR MUST NOT CONTAIN AN LLM

The supervisor cannot call a model to decide:

    "What should happen?"

Its decisions must be explicit finite policy.

Example:

    IF project.complete == false
    AND paused == false
    AND director heartbeat stale
    AND no active director process
    AND relaunch cooldown expired

    THEN launch configured director process

Not:

    ask model whether director should be restarted.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
9.6 SUPERVISOR RETRY POLICY

Retry policy concerns LIVENESS and provider state.

It should not directly attempt to fix code.

Examples:

    director process exit
        -> restart according to policy

    manager lease stale
        -> emit recovery directive to Meta Luna

    free provider repeated transport failure
        -> mark route degraded
        -> enforce cooldown
        -> inform Meta Luna

    test failure
        -> do NOT autonomously reinterpret code
        -> project remains unfinished
        -> Meta Luna handles next engineering action

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
9.7 SYSTEMD INSTALLATION

Use a dedicated systemd service.

Discover correct Node/runtime paths.

Do not blindly paste example absolute paths.

Service should include appropriate:

    User
    WorkingDirectory
    ExecStart
    Restart
    RestartSec
    environment/config path

Use security hardening compatible with required Codex auth/cache.

Do not disable hardening globally because one path needs to be writable.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
9.8 START ON BOOT

Enable the supervisor.

Verify:

    systemctl status
    process exists
    log output
    config loaded
    project registered

If user-level systemd is used:

verify it actually survives logout/restart under the machine's current
configuration.

Do not assume.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
9.9 DETAILED SUPERVISOR ACCEPTANCE TESTS

TEST SUP-001 — CLEAN START

Procedure:

1. stop supervisor cleanly;
2. ensure no duplicate instance;
3. start service;
4. inspect log;
5. inspect state file.

Expected:

- exactly one supervisor;
- configuration valid;
- no crash loop;
- target project loaded.

Failure blocks bootstrap.

TEST SUP-002 — DIRECTOR LAUNCH

Procedure:

1. mark project unfinished;
2. remove active director safely;
3. allow supervisor cycle.

Expected:

- supervisor identifies missing director;
- launches exactly one;
- records process identity;
- heartbeat begins.

Failure blocks bootstrap.

TEST SUP-003 — DIRECTOR DEATH RECOVERY

Procedure:

1. checkpoint Meta Luna;
2. kill director process intentionally;
3. leave completion gate false.

Expected:

- supervisor notices stale/dead process;
- does not declare project complete;
- observes cooldown;
- launches replacement;
- replacement resumes same project.

Also verify:

- no duplicate root project;
- no duplicate Kanban board;
- no requirement reset.

TEST SUP-004 — RAPID CRASH LOOP

Procedure:

make test director exit repeatedly.

Expected:

- supervisor does not spin hundreds of processes;
- restart burst limit/cooldown activates;
- directive/error state becomes visible.

TEST SUP-005 — PAUSE

Set project paused.

Kill director.

Expected:

- supervisor does NOT relaunch while paused.

Resume.

Expected:

- normal liveness behavior resumes.

TEST SUP-006 — MANAGER LEASE EXPIRY

Create a synthetic manager lease.

Allow it to expire.

Expected:

- supervisor marks stale;
- creates recovery directive;
- does not itself implement manager's tasks.

TEST SUP-007 — SUPERVISOR RESTART

Restart supervisor.

Expected:

- project state loaded from disk;
- no duplicate state;
- no task loss.

TEST SUP-008 — SYSTEM RESTART PATH

Where safe, simulate/rehearse service restart order.

Expected:

- supervisor returns;
- waits for required dependencies if necessary;
- resumes monitoring.

TEST SUP-009 — FALSE COMPLETION DEFENSE

Stop every AI worker but keep release gate false.

Expected:

- project remains incomplete;
- supervisor eventually ensures Meta Luna returns.

TEST SUP-010 — TRUE COMPLETION

Use a TEST PROJECT fixture with completion conditions satisfied.

Expected:

- supervisor does not unnecessarily relaunch director after durable complete
  state.

Do not use the real project to fake completion.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
9.10 SUPERVISOR TEST EVIDENCE

For each acceptance test store:

    test_id
    timestamp
    setup
    command
    expected
    observed
    exit/result
    logs
    relevant state files
    reviewer

No one-word:

    PASS

without evidence.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
9.11 SUPERVISOR BOOTSTRAP GATE

SUPERVISOR_BOOTSTRAP_GATE = PASS only when:

- source inspected;
- tests pass;
- service installed;
- starts automatically;
- real Meta Luna launcher verified;
- heartbeat works;
- death recovery demonstrated;
- pause demonstrated;
- manager stale recovery demonstrated;
- duplicate prevention demonstrated;
- completion conditions demonstrated on fixture;
- no paid credentials required for liveness.

Only then may broad product execution begin.


===============================================================================
SECTION 10 — META-LUNA LIFECYCLE, HEARTBEATS, CHECKPOINTS, AND RESUME SEMANTICS
===============================================================================

10.1 LUNA IS A ROLE, NOT A PROCESS

Never design around:

    one infinitely long conversation.

A Meta Luna invocation is an execution episode.

It may end.

That is normal.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
10.2 DIRECTOR INSTANCE IDENTITY

Each Meta Luna invocation receives:

    project_id
    director_instance_id
    started_at
    parent_supervisor_generation
    current_git_sha
    checkpoint_version

Do not treat `director_instance_id` as the project identity.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
10.3 STARTUP PROCEDURE

Every Meta Luna launch begins with recovery.

It must NOT immediately invent new tasks.

Read durable sources in a prescribed order.

Suggested order:

1. controller contract;
2. supervisor directive queue;
3. BUILD_STATE.json;
4. current Part A/Part B checksums;
5. requirements traceability;
6. current workstreams;
7. current leases;
8. provider/model health;
9. blockers;
10. latest decisions;
11. evidence manifest;
12. Kanban state;
13. Git/worktrees;
14. active processes/services.

Only then choose next action.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
10.4 HEARTBEAT CONTENT

Heartbeat should include enough diagnostic information to determine whether the
director is alive and making progress.

Example fields:

    timestamp
    project_id
    director_instance_id
    current_phase
    current_activity
    last_successful_action
    next_action
    active_manager_ids
    open_blockers_count
    release_gate_state
    git_sha

Heartbeat must not contain secrets.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
10.5 HEARTBEAT FREQUENCY

Choose a sensible interval.

It must be significantly shorter than stale timeout.

Example concept:

    heartbeat every 30–60 seconds when actively orchestrating

    stale threshold several heartbeat intervals later

Do not declare Luna dead because one long command took 10 seconds.

The prior architecture's short inactivity assumptions must not be repeated.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
10.6 LONG-RUNNING COMMANDS

If Meta Luna launches a deterministic command expected to take a while:

- supervisor should not confuse absence of model output with director death;
- heartbeat helper/process should remain valid where technically appropriate;
- or stale timeout should accommodate command duration.

Do not classify:

    silence

as:

    completion.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
10.7 CHECKPOINT FREQUENCY

Checkpoint after meaningful state transitions.

Examples:

- architecture gate passed;
- new workstream created;
- manager launched;
- review rejection;
- merge completed;
- provider route demoted;
- major blocker encountered;
- phase transition.

Also checkpoint before anticipated context exhaustion or voluntary exit.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
10.8 CHECKPOINT CONTENT

A checkpoint should include:

    checkpoint_id
    timestamp
    project_phase
    protocol hashes
    current Git integration SHA
    workstream states
    active tasks
    manager leases
    open reviews
    known failures
    provider states
    evidence gaps
    next dependency frontier
    unresolved decisions
    human blockers
    next recommended actions

Do not store raw secrets.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
10.9 RESUME MUST NOT TRUST MODEL MEMORY

A replacement Luna should assume:

    I REMEMBER NOTHING RELIABLY.

It reconstructs state from durable project data.

Even if conversational memory happens to be available, durable state wins.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
10.10 DUPLICATE PREVENTION

Before creating a new:

- project;
- task;
- workstream;
- manager;
- board;

check whether an equivalent durable object already exists.

Use stable IDs and idempotency keys where practical.

Example:

    workstream_id = WS-LIVING-OFFICE

not:

    "new-living-office-workstream-7"

after every restart.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
10.11 RECOVERY DIRECTIVES

Supervisor directives should be narrow.

Examples:

    DIRECTOR_RESTARTED

    MANAGER_LEASE_STALE: WS-UI

    PROVIDER_CIRCUIT_OPEN: openrouter/inkling-free

    PROJECT_PAUSED

    RELEASE_GATE_STILL_FALSE

Meta Luna decides engineering response.

Supervisor does not.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
10.12 VOLUNTARY LUNA EXIT

Meta Luna is allowed to end a session.

Before doing so:

- flush current decisions;
- checkpoint;
- update heartbeat;
- ensure no in-memory-only task exists;
- ensure active manager leases are durable;
- ensure any important worker result has been stored.

The supervisor may later relaunch.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
10.13 SUBSCRIPTION LIMIT EXHAUSTION

If Codex subscription usage temporarily blocks Luna:

do not switch to paid API.

Supervisor should:

- record subscription unavailable;
- apply cooldown;
- continue any deterministic operations that do not require Luna;
- allow active free workers where safe;
- retry subscription route later.

Do not lose project state.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
10.14 META-LUNA RECOVERY TEST

Perform a controlled recovery after real project state exists.

Test:

- create workstream;
- launch manager;
- checkpoint;
- terminate Meta Luna;
- allow supervisor relaunch;
- verify replacement reconstructs workstream and manager state;
- verify it does NOT duplicate task graph;
- verify next action is consistent.

Store evidence.

This test is mandatory before trusting multi-day autonomy.


