SECTION 11 — HERMES DURABLE EXECUTION SUBSTRATE, KANBAN, DISPATCHER, CREWS, AND CONDUCTOR
===============================================================================

11.1 INSPECT INSTALLED HERMES

Do not assume current public documentation exactly matches local installation.

Inspect:

    Hermes version
    Hermes Studio version
    gateway version
    profile commands
    Kanban capabilities
    dispatcher behavior
    database/storage paths
    review states
    worktree support
    goal-mode support
    profile routing behavior
    Crew implementation
    Conductor implementation

Record source/command evidence.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
11.2 BUILD KANBAN AUTHORITY

The build needs one canonical durable task lifecycle.

Prefer native Hermes durable Kanban if local inspection confirms it has the
required capabilities.

Do not blindly assume Studio's visual Tasks board is the same database.

Prove it.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
11.3 UI KANBAN VERIFICATION TEST

Create a disposable test card through one interface.

Observe native storage/other interface.

Modify status safely.

Determine whether:

    same identity
    same database
    same lifecycle
    same review state

are actually shared.

Record result.

If they are separate:

select one canonical authority.

Do not sync two mutable authorities bidirectionally without a compelling
architecture reason.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
11.4 ONE DISPATCHER

Exactly one dispatcher owns worker claims on the canonical board.

If the gateway embeds dispatcher functionality:

use that mechanism if locally appropriate.

Do not simultaneously run:

    gateway dispatcher
    standalone legacy dispatcher
    custom supervisor dispatcher

against the same tasks.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
11.5 SUPERVISOR RELATIONSHIP

Supervisor watches:

    Is Meta Luna alive?
    Is project unfinished?
    Are leases stale?

It does NOT:

    claim code cards.

Kanban dispatcher handles worker assignment.

This separation must be tested.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
11.6 PROFILE ROLE

Profiles are execution lanes.

Profile descriptions should affect routing only according to actual installed
behavior.

For each profile eventually define:

    stable ID
    routing description
    SOUL/system instruction
    allowed tools
    denied tools
    workspace policy
    model policy
    reviewer policy
    data/privacy policy
    what it must never do

Part B expands role profiles.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
11.7 CREWS

Crews exist primarily for:

- human visibility;
- convenient group invocation;
- reusable team views.

They do not replace:

- departments;
- build workstreams;
- Kanban.

If Crews have a member limit:

design multiple coherent crews rather than duplicating agent identities.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
11.8 CONDUCTOR

Conductor may be valuable for:

- bounded temporary mission orchestration;
- visualization;
- short multi-worker subtasks.

Do not use Conductor mission lifetime as:

    persistent employee lifetime.

Do not use temporary Conductor state as:

    global build completion state.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
11.9 GOAL MODE

If Hermes supports goal-mode cards:

use only where a bounded task benefits from repeated internal iteration.

Example:

    "Fix this E2E failure until these 4 exact assertions pass."

Do not put entire program into one never-ending `/goal`.

Root project state lives durably outside a single model session.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
11.10 KANBAN TASK CONTRACT

Every build task should eventually contain sufficient fields such as:

    task_id
    requirement_ids
    workstream
    objective
    context
    dependencies
    file ownership
    acceptance criteria
    test requirements
    evidence requirements
    implementer class
    reviewer class
    retry count
    current state
    result commit

Part B provides detailed card template.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
11.11 BUILD STATES

Use a state model that distinguishes:

    BACKLOG
    READY
    QUEUED
    IN_PROGRESS
    WAITING_DEPENDENCY
    WAITING_PROVIDER
    WAITING_REVIEW
    CHANGES_REQUESTED
    BLOCKED_HUMAN
    FAILED
    APPROVED
    INTEGRATED
    CANCELLED

Adapt to actual Hermes state names while retaining semantic distinctions.

Do not collapse:

    provider waiting
    implementation failure
    human blocker

into one generic "blocked."

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
11.12 DEPENDENCY SAFETY

Do not allow downstream implementation to invent contracts while an upstream
architecture task is unresolved.

Examples:

    event schema before multiple UI workers depend on it

    Project/Task/Run identity before dashboards bind to it

    office-state adapter contract before animation worker hardcodes states

Use dependencies.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
11.13 DISPATCH CONCURRENCY

Start conservatively.

For example:

    max active Luna managers: 2

    max implementation worker per overlapping file region: 1

    total free workers:
        based on VPS resources + provider quota

Increase only after observing stability.

Do not create 30 workers simply because spawning them is possible.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
11.14 KANBAN RECOVERY TEST

Test:

- create card;
- worker claims;
- kill worker;
- restart gateway if safe;
- verify card remains;
- verify state understandable;
- verify replacement can continue;
- verify history preserved.

Failure blocks confidence in unattended execution.


